WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.660 --> 00:00:03.730
YUFENG GUO: Natural Language
processing is not an easy task.

00:00:03.730 --> 00:00:06.000
And when it comes to
domain-specific language,

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:07.710
things get even trickier.

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:09.960
Standard pre-trained
benchmark models

00:00:09.960 --> 00:00:14.380
aren't always useful in
industry-specific applications.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:17.220
So how can we train a
text classification system

00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:18.840
on custom data?

00:00:18.840 --> 00:00:20.130
Stay tuned to find out.

00:00:28.910 --> 00:00:32.420
Welcome to AI Adventures, where
we explore the art, science,

00:00:32.420 --> 00:00:34.310
and tools of machine learning.

00:00:34.310 --> 00:00:35.560
My name is Yufeng Guo.

00:00:35.560 --> 00:00:37.330
And on this episode,
we're going to look

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:40.150
at how to use AutoML
natural language

00:00:40.150 --> 00:00:42.790
to build a custom
text classifier,

00:00:42.790 --> 00:00:46.630
using a dataset
from Stack Overflow.

00:00:46.630 --> 00:00:49.160
Stack Overflow is an
incredibly useful tool.

00:00:49.160 --> 00:00:52.210
And it has saved me
on many occasions.

00:00:52.210 --> 00:00:55.210
But sometimes it gets
tedious tagging questions.

00:00:55.210 --> 00:00:56.980
And I wish it
would automatically

00:00:56.980 --> 00:01:00.490
generate or at least
suggest some tags when

00:01:00.490 --> 00:01:01.900
posting a question.

00:01:01.900 --> 00:01:05.290
So today, we will try to
build a machine learning model

00:01:05.290 --> 00:01:08.680
for recommending tags for
Stack Overflow questions

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:10.942
about machine learning.

00:01:10.942 --> 00:01:13.725
To get started, we're going
to need some training data.

00:01:13.725 --> 00:01:16.830
I have spoken about the BigQuery
datasets program before,

00:01:16.830 --> 00:01:19.260
and it comes to the
rescue here again.

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:23.400
With over 25 gigabytes of
Stack Overflow questions here,

00:01:23.400 --> 00:01:27.080
I think we've got enough
material to work with.

00:01:27.080 --> 00:01:30.170
Using AutoML Natural Language
is quite convenient for our use

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:32.990
case because I don't
necessarily know

00:01:32.990 --> 00:01:35.120
what I want to build initially.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:37.670
It would be great to be
able to easily experiment

00:01:37.670 --> 00:01:39.810
with a couple of
different options.

00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:41.570
So to start out, let's
just try building

00:01:41.570 --> 00:01:44.570
a simple classification model
that just differentiates

00:01:44.570 --> 00:01:48.080
between whether a question
is asking about TensorFlow

00:01:48.080 --> 00:01:50.750
or other, something else.

00:01:50.750 --> 00:01:53.720
The SQL query we're going to
use to pre-process this data,

00:01:53.720 --> 00:01:55.190
it's a little bit involved.

00:01:55.190 --> 00:01:57.770
We'll combine the title and
the text fields together

00:01:57.770 --> 00:02:00.410
into one long string
of text, strip out

00:02:00.410 --> 00:02:03.380
all that HTML
formatting tag nonsense.

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:04.520
We don't need that.

00:02:04.520 --> 00:02:09.509
And we'll replace those vertical
bars in the tags with commas.

00:02:09.509 --> 00:02:11.570
So we'll limit our
questions to only

00:02:11.570 --> 00:02:15.560
be those that are tagged with
TensorFlow, Keras, Matplotlib,

00:02:15.560 --> 00:02:18.380
Pandas, and Scikit Learn.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.990
That way it's just
machine learning topics.

00:02:20.990 --> 00:02:23.780
So at this point, you might be
wondering if we shouldn't just

00:02:23.780 --> 00:02:26.210
make a tag recommendation
system by writing,

00:02:26.210 --> 00:02:27.890
say, an IF statement.

00:02:27.890 --> 00:02:30.060
In some cases, you'd be right.

00:02:30.060 --> 00:02:32.450
But there's definitely
still questions out there

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:35.540
which don't mention the word
TensorFlow, even though they

00:02:35.540 --> 00:02:37.970
are about TensorFlow.

00:02:37.970 --> 00:02:40.790
So to even things out
and prevent the model

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:44.750
from using the word TensorFlow
to basically cheat and find

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.640
those questions
about TensorFlow,

00:02:46.640 --> 00:02:50.510
let's replace all of the
obvious words with, let's say,

00:02:50.510 --> 00:02:52.550
the word avocado.

00:02:52.550 --> 00:02:56.580
For your work model, you
can choose a different food.

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.510
Now that our Stack
Overflow questions are all

00:02:59.510 --> 00:03:03.290
about avocado datasets
and avocado classifiers,

00:03:03.290 --> 00:03:05.630
we can use AutoML
Natural Language

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:08.480
to train a model that
decides whether a question is

00:03:08.480 --> 00:03:11.900
about TensorFlow
or something else.

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:14.780
Training AutoML Natural Language
is very straightforward.

00:03:14.780 --> 00:03:16.940
And you just click Train.

00:03:16.940 --> 00:03:19.290
It seems to have yielded
good results for us.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:21.770
And we can test it with
a question from outside

00:03:21.770 --> 00:03:22.980
of our training set.

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:25.490
Here's one about
the .net framework.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.430
And the model seems to correctly
recognize that it is not

00:03:28.430 --> 00:03:29.840
about TensorFlow.

00:03:29.840 --> 00:03:31.040
Great.

00:03:31.040 --> 00:03:33.110
So now we can look at
something more useful

00:03:33.110 --> 00:03:36.290
than just TensorFlow or
not, labeling the questions

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:39.680
with up to five different
tags all about those different

00:03:39.680 --> 00:03:41.150
machine learning libraries.

00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:43.650
We can use our dataset,
just as before,

00:03:43.650 --> 00:03:47.120
and replace the library
names with avocado.

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:50.030
And we'll need to update
the labels so that instead

00:03:50.030 --> 00:03:53.480
of just TensorFlow and other,
we have all five libraries

00:03:53.480 --> 00:03:54.410
represented.

00:03:54.410 --> 00:03:57.560
And we can see in
AutoML Natural Language

00:03:57.560 --> 00:04:00.060
that the datasets
are roughly balanced.

00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.600
And once we wait for the
training to complete,

00:04:02.600 --> 00:04:05.540
we actually have quite some
good results from the training.

00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:07.340
I was very happy with this.

00:04:07.340 --> 00:04:11.330
AutoML Natural Language shows
not just the training scores,

00:04:11.330 --> 00:04:14.420
but it also shows false
positives and false negatives

00:04:14.420 --> 00:04:16.829
for each of those five classes.

00:04:16.829 --> 00:04:20.029
So this helps us spot potential
errors in our data or errors

00:04:20.029 --> 00:04:21.170
in the model.

00:04:21.170 --> 00:04:23.540
We can try a couple of
different sample questions

00:04:23.540 --> 00:04:26.840
and paste them into the
Predict tab of the interface

00:04:26.840 --> 00:04:30.020
and see if those tags and
the scores are reasonable.

00:04:30.020 --> 00:04:34.040
And in our case, they seem to
have turned out pretty well.

00:04:34.040 --> 00:04:37.500
AutoML Natural Language allowed
us to quickly and easily test

00:04:37.500 --> 00:04:41.130
out some ideas about our data
with different ways of labeling

00:04:41.130 --> 00:04:43.030
and training that model.

00:04:43.030 --> 00:04:45.180
We didn't have to deal
with tokenization,

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:48.030
word representation
models, or embeddings.

00:04:48.030 --> 00:04:50.850
All we needed to do was
have an understanding

00:04:50.850 --> 00:04:53.970
of our dataset and the task
we were hoping to achieve,

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:56.880
and then see if the system
could make a model that

00:04:56.880 --> 00:04:59.280
was suitable for our use case.

00:04:59.280 --> 00:05:02.220
This meant that we could focus
on the problem we are trying

00:05:02.220 --> 00:05:05.550
to solve, tagging Stack
Overflow questions,

00:05:05.550 --> 00:05:08.490
rather than figuring
out how to model text.

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.650
Now that we know it's
possible to build this model

00:05:11.650 --> 00:05:14.850
and that the data is there,
we can weigh the trade off

00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.400
between building a
custom model ourselves

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.670
versus just using the
model that we already made

00:05:20.670 --> 00:05:23.410
from AutoML Natural Language.

00:05:23.410 --> 00:05:26.160
Thanks for watching this
episode of Cloud AI Adventures.

00:05:26.160 --> 00:05:28.230
And if you enjoyed it,
click that Like button

00:05:28.230 --> 00:05:31.430
and be sure to subscribe to get
all the latest episodes right

00:05:31.430 --> 00:05:32.800
when they come out.

